      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Dugspur’s name honors Charles Carroll of Carrollton, the last surviving signer of the Declaration of Independence, rather than John Carroll himself.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/dugspur_virginia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early settlers were Scotch‑Irish immigrants who arrived after the Revolutionary War, drawn by high‑altitude land grants despite tough soils.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/dugspur_virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The area’s first inhabitants were Native Americans who lived there for about 10,000 years before European arrival.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/dugspur_virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Dugspur emerged as a distinct settlement in the late 18th century, closely tied to Quaker communities that promoted mutual aid and anti‑slavery sentiments.",
